WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 163: William Howard Taft and the Ballinger-Pinchot Feud: The Break with TR (1909–1912)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.680
[Rebecca] Today, we conclude our portrait of William Howard Taft by investigating the epic environmental and personal scandal that destroyed his administration.

4
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:27.1000
[Rebecca] The Ballinger Pinchot feud pitted legal formalism against crusading conservationism, severing the bond between Taft and Theodore Roosevelt, and setting the stage for the historic election of 1912.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.040
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

6
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.040
[George] Roosevelt was an activist president who viewed the law as a flexible instrument to achieve moral ends.

7
00:00:49.040 --> 00:01:00.960
[George] If the public welfare required protecting millions of acres of Western timberland, TR believed the president had the inherent constitutional authority to act unless expressly forbidden by a statute.

8
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.440
[George] Backslash and backslash and Taft, by contrast, was a strict constructionist federal judge.

9
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:16.320
[George] Taft believed with equal moral conviction that the executive branch could exercise only those powers explicitly granted by Congress in written law.

10
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:31.440
[George] When Roosevelt left office in March 1909, his beloved chief forester, Gifford Pinchot, the charismatic father of the United States Forest Service, remained in office, fiercely determined to guard TR's conservation legacy.

11
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.360
[George] But when Taft appointed Richard A.

12
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:44.240
[George] Ballinger, an unyielding Seattle corporate attorney as Secretary of the Interior, a collision between two irreconcilable visions of government became inevitable.

13
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:55.840
[George] Backslash and backslash and Taft's reverence for the separation of powers led him to believe that executive discretion must always operate strictly within the boundaries established by congressional statute.

14
00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:05.960
[George] While Roosevelt acted boldly whenever the law was silent, Taft insisted that executive action required explicit statutory authorization from Congress.

15
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:17.560
[George] Backslash and backslash and this foundational legal divide between TR's sweeping stewardship theory and Taft's strict constitutionalism made conflict between their loyal followers inevitable.

16
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:24.200
[George] The spark that ignited the controversy lay buried in the frozen wilderness of the Controller Bay region of Alaska.

17
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:32.760
[George] A group of speculators known as the Cunningham Syndicate had filed claims on 5,000 acres of extraordinarily rich anthracite coal lands.

18
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:41.160
[George] Under federal land laws, individuals were permitted to claim only small, independent parcels of coal land for personal use.

19
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.600
[George] Combining claims into corporate monopolies was illegal.

20
00:02:44.600 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] Backslash and backslash and federal investigators uncovered evidence that the Cunningham claimants had signed a secret agreement with the Morgan Guggenheim Syndicate, a colossal corporate mining and railroad trust known in the West as the Alaska Syndicate, to consolidate the coal fields into a massive corporate monopoly.

21
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:14.520
[George] When Ballinger had served as land office commissioner under Roosevelt, he had initially favored the Cunningham claims, and upon leaving office, had briefly represented the syndicate as a private attorney.

22
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:27.400
[George] When Balinger returned as Secretary of the Interior under Taft, he ordered the land office to expedite the patenting of the Cunningham claims, overruling a young, idealistic federal investigator named Louis R.

23
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:40.680
[George] Glavis, backslash and backslash and the Cunningham Coal claims in Alaska highlighted the immense commercial value of Western public lands and the intense pressure from Eastern Capital to exploit natural resources.

24
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:50.400
[George] The Morgan Guggenheim Consortium sought to construct deep water ports, railways, and coal mines, threatening to monopolize Alaska's energy wealth.

25
00:03:50.640 --> 00:04:02.720
[George] Backslash and backslash and progressive conservationists warned that if the Cunningham claims were certified, the public domain would be stripped of its richest mineral wealth for the enrichment of Wall Street syndicates.

26
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:15.440
[George] Louis Glavis, a 30-year-old land office field investigator, was convinced that Ballinger was actively conspiring to hand over billions of dollars worth of Alaskan coal to Wall Street robber barons.

27
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:22.880
[George] Terrified that his superiors would bury the evidence, Glavis traveled to Idaho to meet privately with Gifford Pinchot.

28
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] Pinchot was outraged, he arranged for Glavis to travel directly to Beverly, Massachusetts, where President Taft was spending his summer vacation.

29
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:42.320
[George] Backslash and Backslash N Glavis presented Taft with a 50-page dossier alleging corruption, conflicts of interest, and fraud by Secretary Ballinger.

30
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.560
[George] Taft took the dossier seriously, reviewing every document alongside Attorney General George W.

31
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.840
[George] Wickersham.

32
00:04:49.840 --> 00:05:00.800
[George] After two weeks of careful legal examination, Judge Taft concluded that while Ballinger may have exercised poor judgment, there was zero evidence of criminal corruption or bribery.

33
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.880
[George] Ballinger had merely insisted on following the strict statutory procedures established by Congress.

34
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:21.840
[George] On September 13, 1909, Taft issued a comprehensive public letter completely exonerating Ballinger, and authorized the dismissal of Louis Glavis for making unfounded defamatory charges against a cabinet secretary.

35
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:32.280
[George] Backslash and backslash and Louis Glavis was an idealistic young civil servant who believed that his highest loyalty belonged to the public interest rather than bureaucratic hierarchy.

36
00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:40.760
[George] When superiors ordered him to expedite the claims, Glavis risked his entire government career to preserve the evidence of corporate collusion.

37
00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:52.920
[George] Backslash and backslash and Glavis's decision to seek out Gifford Pinchot elevated a bureaucratic dispute into an explosive national political scandal that shook the Taft administration to its foundations.

38
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.360
[George] Instead of ending the controversy, Glavis's firing triggered a media firestorm.

39
00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:08.680
[George] Glavis teamed up with prominent muckraking journalist Norman Hapgood, publishing a sensational, explosive article in Collier's weekly titled, The Whitewashing of Ballinger.

40
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.280
[George] Are the Guggenheims in charge of the Department of the Interior?

41
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.160
[George] Millions of Americans were horrified.

42
00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:25.000
[George] It appeared that President Taft was systematically undoing Theodore Roosevelt's sacred conservation legacy and selling public lands to predatory corporate syndicates.

43
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.600
[George] Backslash and backslash and Gifford Pinchot decided to force the issue into open warfare.

44
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:43.720
[George] Although Taft repeatedly warned Pinchot to cease his public attacks, Pinchot wrote an insubordinate letter to Senator Jonathan Dolover of Iowa, openly accusing Balinger of corruption and criticizing the president.

45
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:50.440
[George] Dolliver read the letter aloud on the floor of the United States Senate on January 6, 1910.

46
00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.360
[George] Taft was deeply pained.

47
00:06:52.360 --> 00:07:03.080
[George] He loved Pinchot as a close personal friend and reappointed him, but no president could tolerate such open defiance from a subordinate without abdicating executive authority.

48
00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:10.520
[George] On January 7, 1910, President Taft signed the order dismissing Gifford Pinchot as chief forrester.

49
00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:21.440
[George] Backslash and backslash and Pinchot's open defiance of the president forced Taft into an agonizing choice between personal friendship and the constitutional authority of the executive office.

50
00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:31.120
[George] Taft spent sleepless nights agonizing over the decision, knowing that firing TR's closest friend would trigger an irreparable political rupture.

51
00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:44.000
[George] Backslash and backslash and yet Taft recognized that allowing a subordinate bureaucrat to publicly insubordinate cabinet secretaries and appeal to Congress would destroy presidential authority and institutional discipline.

52
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.800
[George] Congress immediately established a joint investigating committee to hold public hearings into the Interior Department.

53
00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.760
[George] What followed was one of the most famous investigative hearings in congressional history.

54
00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:06.560
[George] Representing Glavis and Colliers Weekly was a brilliant, razorsharp Boston reform attorney named Louis Dembit's Brandeis, the future legendary Supreme Court Justice.

55
00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.160
[George] Backslash and backslash and Brandeis's cross-examinations were forensic masterpieces.

56
00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:18.640
[George] Through surgical questioning of administration clerks, Brandeis uncovered a major executive deception.

57
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:27.760
[George] Attorney General Wickersham had backdated his official legal report exonerating Balinger to make it appear that Taft had reviewed it before clearing the secretary.

58
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:34.240
[George] And the administration had attempted to conceal the fact that a presidential aide had ghost-written part of the decision.

59
00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:44.640
[George] While the committee's conservative Republican majority formally exonerated Ballinger by a party line vote in December 1910, Brandeis won the battle of public opinion.

60
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:53.280
[George] Ballinger's public reputation was completely ruined, and he was forced to resign from the cabinet in disgrace in March 1911.

61
00:08:53.280 --> 00:09:04.920
[George] Backslash and backslash and Louis Brandeis's investigative brilliance during the congressional hearings transformed the dispute into a national referendum on government transparency and corporate influence.

62
00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:15.720
[George] Through relentless, forensic questioning, Brandeis proved that public land administration required rigorous public oversight rather than backroom bureaucratic decisions.

63
00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.920
[George] Backslash and backslash and the hearings captivated the American public for months.

64
00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:28.520
[George] Turning Louis Brandees into a national progressive hero and severely tarnishing the reputation of Secretary Ballinger.

65
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:33.160
[George] The personal tragedy of the scandal occurred thousands of miles away in Europe.

66
00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:46.200
[George] In April 1910, Gifford Pinchot boarded a luxury oceanliner and steamed to Italy, meeting Theodore Roosevelt at Porto Morizio on the Italian Riviera just as TR emerged from the African wilderness.

67
00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:53.160
[George] For hours, Pinchot walked with Roosevelt through olive groves, pouring his bitter grievances into TR's ear.

68
00:09:53.160 --> 00:10:04.280
[George] Backslash and backslash and Pinchot convinced Roosevelt that Taft had surrendered to Conservative Party bosses, dismantled progressive conservation, and betrayed TR's legacy.

69
00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:07.000
[George] Roosevelt was heartbroken and enraged.

70
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:16.680
[George] When Roosevelt returned to New York in June, he refused to visit the White House, agreeing only to a brief, painfully awkward meeting with Taft at Beverly.

71
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:23.080
[George] The warmth, laughter, and brotherhood that had united the two men for over a decade had vanished forever.

72
00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:28.360
[George] The stage was set for Roosevelt's fateful challenge to Taft in 1912.

73
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:39.400
[George] Backslash and backslash and Pinchot's meeting with Roosevelt in Italy permanently poisoned T.R.'s perception of Taft, convincing Roosevelt that his progressive legacy was being dismantled.

74
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.120
[George] Walking through the Italian olive groves.

75
00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:48.240
[George] Pinchot presented a one-sided indictment that painted Taft as a tool of reactionary corporate bosses.

76
00:10:48.480 --> 00:11:00.160
[George] Backslash and Backslash and Roosevelt absorbed Pinchot's grievances deeply, feeling an acute personal responsibility to return to public life and defend the progressive principles he had championed.

77
00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:06.240
[George] The Ballinger Pinchot controversy was a tragedy of good men trapped in an ideological collision.

78
00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:12.480
[George] In reality, modern environmental historians have largely vindicated William Howard Taft.

79
00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.040
[George] Far from destroying conservation.

80
00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:34.240
[George] Taft's administration established more permanent national forest reserves, protected more public mineral lands, and signed more conservation legislation, including the Historic Weeks Act of 1911, which created the national forests of the Eastern United States than almost any president in history.

81
00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:45.920
[George] Backslash and backslash and furthermore, Taft cancelled the fraudulent Cunningham Coal claims himself, ensuring that not an acre of Alaskan coal was ever handed to the Morgan Guggenheim Syndicate.

82
00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.800
[George] Yet the political damage was irrevocable.

83
00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:58.960
[George] The controversy fractured the Republican Party into two warring factions, paving the way for the Bull Moose split of 1912 and handing the White House to Woodrow Wilson.

84
00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:04.720
[George] Most poignantly, it permanently severed the friendship between Taft and Theodore Roosevelt.

85
00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:15.600
[George] It was not until 1918, in the dining room of the Hotel Blackstone in Chicago, that the two aging statesmen accidentally met, embraced in tears and reconciled.

86
00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:29.920
[George] Just months before Roosevelt's untimely death, backslash and backslash and Taft's ultimate cancellation of the Alaskan coal claims vindicated his conservation record, even as the political fallout permanently fractured the Republican Party.

87
00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:40.840
[George] Modern historians recognize that Taft protected millions of acres of Western lands, acting as a dedicated environmental steward despite the bitter attacks of his critics.

88
00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:54.680
[George] Backslash and backslash and the tragic estrangement between Taft and Roosevelt deprived the nation of their combined leadership, leaving both men with deep personal sorrow until their reconciliation in Chicago years later.

89
00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:14.840
[Rebecca] By splitting the Republican Party, it brought an end to 16 years of Republican rule and paved the way for the transformative presidency of Woodrow Wilson.

90
00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:29.160
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn our attention to Wilson, the Princeton scholar who arrived in Washington with his New Freedom Agenda, created the Federal Reserve, enacted the Federal Income Tax, and led America into the crucible of World War I.

91
00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.560
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

